BLOCK|h1|1|Cluster-lang: Chapter 12 — Machine Adaptability
BLOCK|p||This chapter explores Cluster-lang's unique **Machine Adaptability**—the ability of a single source file to target and run across completely different computing environments, from bare-metal microcontrollers to web browsers and enterprise Linux servers.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Defining Machine Adaptability
BLOCK|p||Most languages are restricted to a specific runtime environment:
BLOCK|list||**Javascript** is optimized for browsers and Node.js servers, but cannot run on bare-metal microcontrollers.
BLOCK|list||**Python** runs on server environments, but is too heavy and slow for low-power chips.
BLOCK|list||**C++** runs anywhere, but requires entirely different codebase layouts, libraries, and compiler toolchains when moving from a Linux server to a microcontroller.
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang solves this by decoupling the language syntax from the target runtime. The same Cluster-lang codebase compiles into different C++ structures depending on the compiler flags passed.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. Compilation Targets
BLOCK|code|mermaid|graph TD
    Source[main.cl Source Code] --> TargetServer[Linux Server Target]
    Source --> TargetMCU[Bare-Metal MCU Target --safe]
    Source --> TargetWASM[Web Browser Target --wasm]
    
    TargetServer --> BinServer[ELF Binary / Web Server]
    TargetMCU --> BinMCU[Arduino / ESP32 / STM32 C++ Code]
    TargetWASM --> BinWASM[WASM + HTML Payload]
BLOCK|h3|3|A. Enterprise Server Target
BLOCK|p||By default, the compiler outputs full C++20 code utilizing multi-threaded thread pools, raw Linux sockets, and standard database interfaces.
BLOCK|list||**Command:** `cluster build main.cl`
BLOCK|list||**Outputs:** Optimized ELF binary.
BLOCK|h3|3|B. Bare-Metal Microcontroller Target (`--safe`)
BLOCK|p||When compiling with the `--safe` flag, the compiler isolates the code from the OS.
BLOCK|list||**Command:** `cluster build main.cl --safe`
BLOCK|list||**Features:**
BLOCK|list||Excludes standard OS headers (sockets, thread pools, file systems).
BLOCK|list||Generates a single, lightweight C++ file (`main.cpp`) and helper header (`cluster_core.hpp`).
BLOCK|list||Enables hardware-level built-ins like `pin_mode(pin, mode)`, `digital_write(pin, value)`, and `attach_interrupt(pin, handler, mode)`.
BLOCK|list||Can be flashed directly onto ESP32, STM32, Arduino, or ARM Cortex chips.
BLOCK|h3|3|C. Web Browser Target (`--wasm`)
BLOCK|p||By passing the `--wasm` flag, the compiler leverages Emscripten toolchains to output browser-ready code.
BLOCK|list||**Command:** `cluster build main.cl --wasm`
BLOCK|list||**Outputs:** HTML, JS, and WebAssembly (`.wasm`) files that run directly inside the browser's sandbox.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Unified Syntax for Diverse Hardware
BLOCK|p||This adaptability allows writing IoT and server systems in a single language.
BLOCK|p||For instance, you can define a data payload schema in a shared file:
BLOCK|code|python|// shared.cl
model SensorData:
    id: int
    value: float
BLOCK|p||And compile it for:
BLOCK|p||1.  **The Sensor Chip (Microcontroller):** Reading physical pins and sending values via serial interface in `--safe` mode.
BLOCK|p||2.  **The Edge Gateway (Raspberry Pi/Linux):** Aggregating inputs and serving a dashboard using `serve_web`.
BLOCK|p||3.  **The Client Console (Browser):** Running interactive data visualizations via WebAssembly.
BLOCK|hr||
